## Decoding the question
**"Elaborate"** asks for a **detailed** explanation. **"Historical context"** means why quotas were adopted at each point; **"evolution"** means how they changed over time; **"key legislation and policies"** means naming the **laws and orders** — the 1935 Act, the constitutions of 1956, 1962 and 1973, the 1985 change, the LFO 2002, the Local Government Ordinance 2001 and the Elections Act 2017. A **timeline** is ideal.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
A **women's quota** reserves a minimum number or share of seats, or candidacies, for women in elected bodies.

#### ii) The argument
Pakistan's quota has evolved from a **symbolic** handful of indirectly filled seats (1956–1988), through a damaging **lapse** (1988–2002), to a **substantial** system since ==2002== — 60 National Assembly seats, provincial and Senate seats, and a candidate quota from ==2017==. It has not yet become **empowering**, because seats remain in party leaders' hands.

### 2. The context: why quotas
Women in South Asia faced exclusion from politics through purdah, low literacy, lack of property and male-dominated parties. Quotas were a way to guarantee a voice. At independence, women had played a visible part in the Pakistan Movement — in processions, the Muslim League's women's committees and refugee relief — and leaders such as Jinnah had spoken of women standing side by side with men. Internationally, **CEDAW** (==1979==, Article 4(1)) recognises **temporary special measures**; Pakistan's **Article 25(3)** allows special provision for women.

### 3. Colonial roots: the 1935 Act
The **Government of India Act 1935** widened women's franchise (to about six million women) and **reserved seats** for women in the legislatures. **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz**, who attended the **Round Table Conferences**, won a seat in the Punjab Assembly in ==1937==. Reserved seats came to Pakistan as part of this inheritance, and with them the habit of treating women as a separate electoral category.

### 4. 1947–1956: the first steps
The **first Constituent Assembly** (==1947==) had only **two** women — **Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Shaista Ikramullah**. The **1956 Constitution** reserved **10 seats** for women — **five each** from East and West Pakistan — for ten years; women could also contest general seats.

### 5. 1962: six seats under Ayub
The **1962 Constitution** of **Ayub Khan** reserved **six seats** (three per wing), filled by **indirect election**. In ==1965== **Fatima Jinnah** challenged Ayub for the presidency — a reminder that women's political ambition went beyond reserved seats.

### 6. 1973: ten seats for a limited period
The **1973 Constitution** reserved **10** National Assembly seats for women for **ten years or three general elections**, whichever was later, again filled indirectly, with some provincial seats. The time limit reflected the view that quotas were **temporary** — a bridge until women could win general seats on their own. History showed that the bridge was removed too soon.

### 7. 1985: twenty seats under Zia
Under **General Zia-ul-Haq**, women's seats were raised to **20** for the ==1985== non-party election. The paradox is sharp: the same regime issued the **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==) and the **Law of Evidence** (==1984==), which harmed women, and provoked the **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==). More seats did not mean more power: the women members of the 1985 Assembly, chosen indirectly in a non-party system, were unable to challenge the discriminatory laws.

::: fig timeline | The evolution of women's quotas in Pakistan.
@layout vertical
1935: Government of India Act — reserved seats for women
1947: First Constituent Assembly — 2 women
1956: Constitution — 10 seats (5 East, 5 West)
1962: Constitution — 6 seats, indirect
1973: Constitution — 10 seats for 10 years or three elections
1985: Raised to 20 seats
1988–2002: Lapse — no reserved seats in 1990, 1993, 1997
2000–01: Devolution Plan and LGO 2001 — 33% local seats
2002: LFO — 60 NA, 17 Senate, 128 provincial seats
2017: Elections Act — 5% tickets; 10% women's turnout rule
:::

### 8. 1988–2002: the lapse and its lesson
The provision **expired** after the ==1988== election. In the elections of ==1990==, ==1993== and ==1997== there were **no reserved seats**, and only **two to six** women won general seats — a National Assembly over **97%** male. This happened even under **Benazir Bhutto**, who promised to restore the seats but lacked the majority or will. The lapse is the strongest historical argument for quotas: without them, women almost vanished.

### 9. 2000–2002: devolution and the LFO

#### i) Local government
**Pervez Musharraf**'s **Devolution of Power Plan** (==2000==) and the **Local Government Ordinance 2001** reserved **33%** of council seats for women; about **36,000** women were elected — the largest entry of women into public office in Pakistan's history, though some seats in conservative districts went unfilled.

#### ii) The Legal Framework Order 2002
The **LFO 2002**, validated by the **17th Amendment** (==2003==), enlarged the National Assembly to **342** and reserved **60** seats (about **17%**), plus **17** in the Senate and **128** in the provinces, filled by **proportional representation** from party lists.

#### iii) Related policies
The **NCSW** (==2000==) and the **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==) formed part of the same package. Women's share of the Assembly jumped from under **3%** to over **20%**. Critics noted that a military ruler had introduced the change, but every elected government since has kept it.

### 10. 2008–2018: consolidation and reform
- **2008:** the cross-party **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** turned numbers into laws (2010–2016).
- **2010:** the **18th Amendment** retained reserved seats and Article 34.
- **2013 onwards:** provincial local government laws kept women's seats but mostly **below 33%**.
- **2017:** the **Elections Act** introduced a **5%** candidate quota (Section 206) and the **10%** women's turnout rule (Section 9).
- **2018:** the **25th Amendment** merged the tribal areas; the National Assembly fell to **336** seats, with **60** still reserved for women.

#### iv) An example of the quota at work
The cross-party **Women's Parliamentary Caucus**, formed in ==2008== under the first woman Speaker, **Dr Fahmida Mirza**, turned the 2002 numbers into laws: on workplace harassment (==2010==), acid attacks and anti-women practices (==2011==), the NCSW Act (==2012==) and honour killing (==2016==). Without the 2002 quota, there would have been no caucus large enough to pass them — the clearest proof that the quota's evolution changed real outcomes for women, not just the numbers in the house.

### 11. Assessment

#### i) Gains
A permanent, larger quota since 2002; a candidate quota since 2017; protection of women's votes; women at about **one-fifth** of the Assembly; and landmark laws passed by women.

#### ii) Gaps
Seats are still filled **indirectly** by party leaders; the 5% rule is weak; only **11** women won general seats in ==2024==; local quotas were cut; and parties treat reserved seats as a **ceiling**. The ==2024–25== dispute over the allocation of reserved seats to PTI-backed independents showed that the system still turns on party arithmetic.

### 12. The next stage and conclusion

#### i) The next stage
**Direct election** of women's seats; a candidate quota of at least **33%** in winnable seats; restored **33%** local quotas; and transparent party lists — following **Nepal**, where constitutional quotas produced about **35%** women in ==2026==.

#### ii) Conclusion
Pakistan's women's quota has evolved from colonial reserved seats, through small indirect quotas in 1956, 1962 and 1973, an increase in 1985 and a damaging lapse, to the substantial system of 2002 and the reforms of 2017. Each stage was shaped by its political context. The quota has moved from **symbolic** to **substantial**; its next evolution must make it **empowering**.

## Why this answer scores
- Covers every **phase** in order, with the **law or policy** behind each.
- Explains the **context** of each change (Ayub, Zia, Musharraf), not just dates.
- Uses the **lapse** as evidence and ends with an **assessment** and next steps.
- A **timeline figure** shows the whole evolution at a glance.
